{"three_classes_of_data": "The object \"three classes of data\" refers to the categorization of the EEG signals within the dataset into three distinct groups: normal (Set B), preictal (Set D), and seizure (Set E). Each class represents a different state or condition related to epilepsy, with 100 EEG signals per class.|Here is another description: The subject refers to the three distinct categories of data utilized in the EEG research conducted by Andrzejak et al. at Bonn University. These categories are crucial for the study as they represent different states of brain activity."},
{"three_classes_of_data__are__normal_set_b_preictal_set_d_and_seizure_set_e": "The predicate 'are' signifies the classification or categorization of the EEG data segments into specific groups based on the type of brain activity they represent. This relation is essential for organizing and analyzing the data effectively."},
{"normal_set_b_preictal_set_d_and_seizure_set_e": "The objects of the triple are the three classes of EEG data: normal (Set B), preictal (Set D), and seizure (Set E). Each class represents a different state of brain activity, with normal data collected from healthy subjects, preictal data from epileptic patients not experiencing a seizure, and seizure data from the same patients during an epileptic episode."},
