{"green_blue_and_red_color__signify__kernel_size_max_pooling_and_fully_connected_layer_respectively": "The predicate \"signify\" in this context indicates that the colors green, blue, and red are used as visual markers to represent different functional layers within the CNN structure. Each color corresponds to a specific type of layer in the network."},
{"kernel_size_max_pooling_and_fully_connected_layer_respectively": "The objects signified by the colors are the kernel size, max-pooling, and fully connected layers, respectively. These layers are critical components of the CNN architecture, each playing a distinct role in processing the input data through the network."},
{"proposed_cnn_architecture": "The proposed CNN architecture is a deep learning model designed specifically for processing EEG signals. It is structured to handle input sample lengths of 4097, with various layers configured to extract and reduce features effectively."},
{"proposed_cnn_architecture__includes__five_convolutional_five_maxpooling_and_three_fully_connected_layers": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the proposed CNN architecture is composed of specific layers. These layers are integral to the functioning of the CNN, each playing a distinct role in processing the input data through convolution, pooling, and full connection operations."},
{"five_convolutional_five_maxpooling_and_three_fully_connected_layers": "The object of the triple consists of five convolutional layers, five maxpooling layers, and three fully connected layers. These layers work together to form an eleven-layer deep CNN, designed to progressively extract and reduce features from the input EEG signals."},
{"stride": "Stride refers to the step size with which the kernel moves across the input data during the convolution and max-pooling operations in a convolutional neural network (CNN). It is a crucial parameter that affects the output dimensions and the amount of information retained from the input data.|Here is another description: Stride is a parameter in convolutional neural networks that determines the step size with which the filter moves across the input signal during the convolution process."},
{"stride__is_set_at__1_and_2_for_convolution_of_the_eeg_signals_and_max_pooling_operations_respectively": "The phrase \"is set at\" indicates the specific configuration or value assigned to the stride parameter within the CNN architecture. This setting determines how the convolution and max-pooling operations are performed on the EEG signals."},
{"1_and_2_for_convolution_of_the_eeg_signals_and_max_pooling_operations_respectively": "The values \"1 and 2\" represent the stride settings for the convolution and max-pooling operations, respectively. A stride of 1 means the kernel moves one unit at a time during convolution, while a stride of 2 means the kernel moves two units at a time during max-pooling, effectively reducing the spatial dimensions of the feature maps."},
{"input_layer_layer_0_in_fig_4": "The input layer, designated as Layer 0 in Figure 4, serves as the initial point in the CNN structure where raw input data is processed. It consists of 4097 input sample lengths and is the starting layer before any convolution or pooling operations are applied."},
{"input_layer_layer_0_in_fig_4__is_convolved_using__equation_3_with_a_kernel_of_size_6_to_produce_layer_1": "The predicate describes the process by which the input layer undergoes convolution. This operation involves applying Equation (3) with a specific kernel size to transform the input data into a feature map, which is essential for extracting relevant features from the input signals."},
{"equation_3_with_a_kernel_of_size_6_to_produce_layer_1": "Equation (3) with a kernel of size 6 represents the specific mathematical operation and parameters used in the convolution process. This convolution is applied to the input layer to produce Layer 1, which is the first feature map in the CNN structure, containing extracted features from the input data."},
{"max_pooling_of_size_2": "Max-pooling of size 2 refers to a downsampling operation in convolutional neural networks (CNNs) where the maximum value within a 2x2 window is selected to reduce the spatial dimensions of the feature maps.|Here is another description: Max-pooling of size 2 is a downsampling technique used in convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to reduce the spatial dimensions of feature maps. It involves selecting the maximum value from a 2x2 window and moving this window across the feature map."},
{"max_pooling_of_size_2__is_applied_to__every_feature_map_layer_2": "The phrase \"is applied to\" indicates the action of performing the max-pooling operation on the specified feature maps within the CNN architecture."},
{"every_feature_map_layer_2": "Every feature map in Layer 2 represents the output of the previous convolutional layer, which undergoes max-pooling to reduce dimensionality and to introduce translational invariance in the CNN model."},
{"number_of_neurons__is_reduced_from__4092_4_to_2046_4": "The reduction in the number of neurons from 4092 \u00d7 4 to 2046 \u00d7 4 occurs due to the application of a max-pooling operation. Max-pooling is a down-sampling process that reduces the dimensionality of the feature maps by selecting the maximum value from portions of the feature map, thereby decreasing the number of neurons."},
{"4092_4_to_2046_4": "The object \"4092 \u00d7 4 to 2046 \u00d7 4\" describes the transformation in the number of neurons after applying a max-pooling operation. Initially, there are 4092 neurons arranged in 4 feature maps. After max-pooling, this is reduced to 2046 neurons in 4 feature maps, indicating a halving of the neuron count in each feature map."},
{"feature_map_in_layer_2": "The feature map in Layer 2 is a representation of the data after the initial convolution and max-pooling operations in the CNN architecture. It serves as the input for further processing in the network."},
{"feature_map_in_layer_2__is_convolved_with__a_kernel_of_size_5_to_produce_layer_3": "The operation \"is convolved with\" refers to the process where the feature map undergoes a convolution operation. This involves applying a mathematical operation using a kernel to transform the feature map into a new representation."},
{"a_kernel_of_size_5_to_produce_layer_3": "A kernel of size 5 is a small matrix used in the convolution process. When convolved with the feature map in Layer 2, it produces Layer 3 by applying the kernel's transformation across the feature map."},
{"max_pooling_operation_of_size_2": "The max-pooling operation of size 2 is a process used in convolutional neural networks to reduce the spatial dimensions of feature maps. It involves selecting the maximum value from a set of values in a defined window, which helps in retaining the most prominent features while reducing the computational load."},
{"max_pooling_operation_of_size_2__is_applied_to__every_feature_map_layer_4": "The phrase \"is applied to\" indicates the action of performing the max-pooling operation on a specific set of data. In this context, it means that the max-pooling process is being executed on the feature maps generated by the previous convolutional layer."},
