{"dataset__contains__three_classes_of_data": "The predicate \"contains\" signifies that the dataset is comprised of or includes specific elements within it. In this context, it indicates the presence of distinct categories or classes of data within the dataset."},
{"three_classes_of_data": "The object \"three classes of data\" refers to the distinct categories within the dataset: normal EEG signals from healthy subjects (Set B), preictal signals from epileptic patients not experiencing a seizure at the time of recording (Set D), and seizure signals from the same epileptic patients during an actual seizure event (Set E).|Here is another description: The subject refers to the three distinct categories of EEG data utilized in the research conducted by Andrzejak et al. at Bonn University. These categories are essential for analyzing and differentiating various states of brain activity."},
{"three_classes_of_data__namely__normal_set_b_preictal_set_d_and_seizure_set_e": "The predicate 'namely' is used to specify and enumerate the three classes of EEG data. It serves as a clarifying conjunction that introduces the detailed classification of the dataset into specific types."},
{"normal_set_b_preictal_set_d_and_seizure_set_e": "The objects are the three specific classes of EEG data: normal (Set B), preictal (Set D), and seizure (Set E). These classes represent different states of brain activity\u2014normal activity from healthy subjects, preictal activity from epileptic patients not currently experiencing a seizure, and seizure activity from the same patients during an epileptic episode."},
{"total": "The subject refers to the total number of EEG signals that are part of the datasets used in the research conducted by Andrzejak et al. at Bonn University."},
{"total__of_eeg_signals_in_each_dataset__100": "The predicate indicates the quantity of EEG signals present within each individual dataset that was analyzed in the research."},
{"each_record": "Each record refers to individual data points within the EEG dataset collected for research purposes. These records are part of a larger collection aimed at studying different states of brain activity, including normal, preictal, and seizure conditions."},
{"each_record__is__a_single_channel_eeg_signal_with_a_duration_of_23_6_s": "The predicate \"is\" serves as a straightforward declaration of the nature or characteristic of each record in the dataset. It establishes the fundamental identity of the subject as a specific type of EEG signal."},
{"a_single_channel_eeg_signal_with_a_duration_of_23_6_s": "A single channel EEG signal with a duration of 23.6 seconds represents the standardized format for each record in the dataset. This uniform duration and channel configuration allow for consistent analysis and comparison across different states of brain activity."},
{"normal_dataset": "The normal dataset is a collection of EEG signals specifically categorized as normal, indicating no seizure activity at the time of recording. It is part of a larger dataset used in research for epilepsy analysis.|Here is another description: The normal dataset consists of EEG signals obtained from 5 healthy subjects, representing a control group in the study to compare against pathological conditions."},
