{"eeg_signal__is_normalized_with__z_score_normalization": "The phrase \"is normalized with\" indicates the process by which the EEG signal data is adjusted to have a standard format. This normalization is crucial for ensuring consistency and comparability across different datasets, especially when they are to be input into machine learning models."},
{"z_score_normalization": "Z-score normalization is a statistical method used to standardize data. It transforms the data so that it has a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. This technique helps in reducing the effect of different scales and allows for better performance of algorithms that are sensitive to the range of input values.|Here is another description: Z-score normalization is a statistical method used to standardize data by transforming it to have a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. This technique is commonly applied in preprocessing steps for machine learning models to ensure that all features contribute equally to the analysis.|Here is another description: Z-score normalization is a statistical method used to standardize data by transforming it to have a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. This technique is commonly applied in preprocessing steps for machine learning models to ensure that all features contribute equally to the analysis."},
{"z_score_normalization__has__zero_mean": "The predicate \"has\" in this context signifies that Z-score normalization inherently includes or results in certain properties. Specifically, it indicates that the normalization process ensures the data has a zero mean."},
{"zero_mean": "Zero mean refers to a statistical property where the average value of a dataset is adjusted to be zero. This is achieved by subtracting the mean of the data from each data point. In the context of Z-score normalization, achieving a zero mean is crucial for standardizing the data before it is input into models like a 1-D deep convolutional network."},
{"z_score_normalization__has__standard_deviation_of_1": "The predicate \"has\" in this context signifies that Z-score normalization inherently includes or results in a specific characteristic, which is a standard deviation of 1. This relationship is fundamental to the definition and application of Z-score normalization."},
{"standard_deviation_of_1": "A standard deviation of 1 is a statistical measure indicating that the data points in a set are spread out by one unit from the mean after normalization. In the context of Z-score normalization, achieving a standard deviation of 1 is a key outcome, ensuring that the data is properly scaled for analysis."},
