{"rectified_linear_activation_unit": "The Rectified Linear Activation Unit (ReLU) is a type of activation function used in neural networks. It outputs the input directly if it is positive, otherwise, it outputs zero. This helps in introducing non-linearity into the model and aids in faster convergence during training.|Here is another description: The rectified linear activation unit is a type of activation function commonly used in neural networks, particularly in deep learning architectures. It is designed to introduce non-linearity into the network, allowing it to learn more complex patterns and features from the input data."},
{"this_work__uses__softmax": "The term \"uses\" indicates that the work incorporates or applies the specified activation functions as part of its process or design."},
{"softmax": "Softmax is an activation function often used in the output layer of neural networks to convert logits into probabilities, ensuring that the sum of the output values is one.|Here is another description: Softmax is a mathematical function used in machine learning and neural networks to convert raw output scores into probabilities. It is particularly useful in multi-class classification problems."},
{"rectified_linear_activation_unit__is_used__after_every_convolutional_layer": "The phrase \"is used\" indicates the application or implementation of the rectified linear activation unit within a specific context or process. In this case, it refers to the practice of employing the activation function after each convolutional layer in a neural network."},
{"after_every_convolutional_layer": "After every convolutional layer refers to the sequence in which operations are performed within a neural network. Convolutional layers are responsible for extracting features from the input data, and the activation function is applied subsequently to introduce non-linearity and enhance the network's learning capabilities."},
{"activation_function": "An activation function is a mathematical operation applied in neural networks to introduce non-linearity, allowing the model to learn complex patterns. It is crucial for transforming the output of a neural network layer into a format that can be used as input for the next layer.|Here is another description: An activation function is a mathematical operation applied to the output of a neural network layer, which maps the output to a set of desired inputs for the next network layer. It is crucial for introducing complexity and enabling the network to learn from data."},
{"activation_function__maps__an_output_to_a_set_of_inputs": "The term \"maps\" in this context refers to the process by which an activation function transforms or assigns an output value based on a set of input values. This mapping is essential for introducing non-linearity and enabling the network to model complex relationships."},
{"an_output_to_a_set_of_inputs": "An output in the context of an activation function refers to the transformed value produced by the function, which is then used as input for subsequent layers in the neural network. A set of inputs represents the collection of values from the previous layer that are processed by the activation function to produce the output."},
{"activation_function__imparts__non_linearity_to_the_network_structure": "The term 'imparts' in this context refers to the action of the activation function introducing or adding a specific property\u2014non-linearity\u2014to the overall structure of the neural network. This property is essential for the network to perform complex computations and learn from varied data inputs."},
{"non_linearity_to_the_network_structure": "Non-linearity in a network structure allows the neural network to model complex patterns and relationships in the data that linear models cannot capture. This property enables the network to perform better on a wide range of tasks by allowing it to fit a broader array of functions."},
{"rectifier_linear_unit": "The Rectifier Linear Unit (ReLU) is a type of activation function commonly used in neural networks, particularly after convolutional layers. It introduces non-linearity into the network, which helps in learning complex patterns."},
{"rectifier_linear_unit__is_an_established_activation_function_for__deep_learning": "\"Is an established activation function for\" indicates that the Rectifier Linear Unit has been widely recognized and adopted as a standard component in the architecture of deep learning models. This predicate highlights its role and significance within the field."},
{"leaky_rectifier_linear_unit_leakyrelu": "The Leaky rectifier linear unit (LeakyRelu) is a variant of the rectified linear unit (ReLU) used as an activation function in neural networks. It introduces nonlinearity and sparsity into the network, enhancing its robustness against small input changes like noise."},
{"leaky_rectifier_linear_unit_leakyrelu__is_used_in_this_work_as_an_activation_function_for_the_convolutional_layers__1_3_5_7_9_11_and_12": "The predicate indicates that the Leaky rectifier linear unit (LeakyRelu) is specifically employed in this work as the activation function following the convolutional layers. This usage is intended to add nonlinearity and improve the network's performance and robustness."},
{"1_3_5_7_9_11_and_12": "The convolutional layers (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, and 12) are specific layers within a neural network architecture where the Leaky rectifier linear unit (LeakyRelu) is applied as an activation function. These layers are responsible for processing input data through convolution operations, and the application of LeakyRelu helps in introducing nonlinearity to the outputs of these layers."},
{"leakyrelu": "LeakyRelu is a type of rectified linear activation function used in deep learning, particularly within convolutional neural networks. It is applied after convolutional layers to introduce specific properties to the network.|Here is another description: LeakyRelu is a type of activation function used in deep learning, specifically within convolutional layers to introduce non-linearity and sparsity into the network structure."},
{"leakyrelu__adds__nonlinearity_and_sparsity_in_the_network_structure": "The predicate \"adds\" signifies that LeakyRelu contributes certain characteristics to the network structure when implemented as an activation function."},
{"nonlinearity_and_sparsity_in_the_network_structure": "Nonlinearity and sparsity are critical attributes introduced by LeakyRelu in the network structure. Nonlinearity allows the network to model complex patterns, while sparsity helps in reducing the number of active neurons, leading to more efficient computation and potentially better generalization."},
{"leakyrelu__provides_robustness_to__small_changes_such_as_noise_in_the_input": "The predicate \"provides robustness to\" indicates that LeakyRelu enhances the resilience of the network against certain types of input variations."},
{"small_changes_such_as_noise_in_the_input": "Small changes such as noise in the input refer to minor, often random variations or errors in the data fed into the neural network, which can affect its performance if not properly managed."},
{"equation_4": "Equation (4) is a mathematical expression presented in the context to define a specific function used in the described deep learning model. It is referenced as the formula that details the operation of the chosen activation function for certain layers in the convolutional network."},
{"equation_4__shows__the_leakyrelu_function": "The predicate \"shows\" indicates that Equation (4) serves as a representation or depiction of the LeakyRelu function. It is the means by which the function's mathematical formulation is communicated to the reader."},
{"the_leakyrelu_function": "The LeakyRelu function is a variant of the Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU) activation function, which introduces nonlinearity and sparsity into the network. It allows a small, non-zero gradient when the unit is not active (i.e., when the input is less than zero), which helps in dealing with the dying ReLU problem and provides robustness against noise in the input data."},
{"softmax__computes__probability_distribution_of_the_k_output_classes": "The predicate \"computes\" indicates that the softmax function performs a calculation to determine the probability distribution. This involves taking the raw output scores and transforming them into a set of probabilities that sum up to one."},
{"probability_distribution_of_the_k_output_classes": "The probability distribution of the k output classes refers to the set of probabilities assigned to each of the k possible classes. In the context of EEG signal classification, these classes could be \"normal,\" \"preictal,\" or \"seizure.\""},
{"layer_13": "Layer 13 is a specific layer within a neural network model designed to classify EEG signals into one of three categories: normal, preictal, or seizure.|Here is another description: Layer 13 is the final layer in a neural network model designed to classify input EEG signals. It is responsible for processing the output from previous layers and applying a specific function to make a prediction."},
{"layer_13__uses__softmax_function": "The predicate \"uses\" indicates that Layer 13 incorporates or applies a specific function or method in its operation to achieve its classification goal."},
{"softmax_function": "The softmax function is a mathematical function used in the final layer of a neural network to convert a raw output vector into a probability distribution. It is essential for multi-class classification problems, as it assigns probabilities to each class, ensuring they sum up to one."},
{"layer_13__predicts__which_class_the_input_eeg_signal_belongs_to": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates the action performed by Layer 13. In this context, it refers to the layer's role in determining the category or class to which the input EEG signal belongs, based on the computed probability distribution."},
{"which_class_the_input_eeg_signal_belongs_to": "The object \"which class the input EEG signal belongs to\" refers to the outcome or result of the prediction made by Layer 13. It specifies the classification of the EEG signal into one of the predefined categories: normal, preictal, or seizure."},
{"input_eeg_signal": "The input EEG signal refers to the electrical activity recorded from the brain, which is used as input data for analysis and classification purposes."},
{"input_eeg_signal__belongs_to__normal_preictal_or_seizure": "The term \"belongs to\" indicates the classification or categorization of the input EEG signal into one of the specified classes based on its characteristics and patterns."},
{"normal_preictal_or_seizure": "The classes \"normal,\" \"preictal,\" and \"seizure\" represent different states or conditions of the EEG signal. \"Normal\" indicates typical brain activity, \"preictal\" signifies the period before a seizure, and \"seizure\" denotes the occurrence of abnormal electrical activity associated with a seizure event."},
