{"table_1": "Table 1 refers to a specific table within a document or study that presents detailed information about a proposed Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) structure. It serves as a comprehensive summary, outlining the various parameters and configurations of the CNN architecture."},
{"table_1__is__the_summary_of_details_of_the_proposed_cnn_structure": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies that Table 1 acts as or represents the summary. It establishes the identity or role of Table 1 within the document, indicating that it encapsulates essential details about the CNN structure."},
{"the_summary_of_details_of_the_proposed_cnn_structure": "The summary of details of the proposed CNN structure refers to a concise and comprehensive overview of the architecture, parameters, and configurations of the Convolutional Neural Network. This summary includes specifics such as the number of layers, types of layers (convolutional, max-pooling, fully connected), kernel sizes, and other relevant parameters that define the CNN's design and functionality."},
{"parameters_kernel_size": "The parameters in question refer to the kernel size used in the convolutional neural network (CNN) structure detailed in Table 1. These parameters are crucial as they determine the size of the convolutional filters applied to the input data."},
{"parameters_kernel_size__are_derived_through__trial_and_error": "The phrase \"are derived through\" indicates the method or process by which the kernel sizes were determined. In this context, it suggests a systematic approach was taken to establish these parameters."},
{"trial_and_error": "Trial-and-error is a fundamental methodological approach where different options are tested to see which one works best. In this scenario, various kernel sizes were experimented with to find the most effective configuration for the CNN structure.|Here is another description: Trial-and-error is a method of obtaining parameters by experimenting with different values and selecting the ones that yield the best performance. This approach involves iteratively testing various parameter settings until an optimal configuration is found."},
{"fig_4": "Fig. 4 is a visual representation that illustrates the detailed architecture of a proposed Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). It serves as a graphical depiction to help understand the structure and flow of the CNN model."},
{"fig_4__shows__the_graphical_representation_of_cnn_structure_with_4097_input_sample_lengths": "The predicate \"shows\" indicates that Fig. 4 provides a visual explanation or representation of the CNN structure. It highlights the various components and their interconnections within the CNN architecture."},
{"the_graphical_representation_of_cnn_structure_with_4097_input_sample_lengths": "The graphical representation of the CNN structure with 4097 input sample lengths details the specific configuration of the CNN, including the number of layers, types of layers (convolutional, max-pooling, and fully connected), and the dimensions of the input and subsequent layers. It uses color coding to distinguish between different types of layers and operations."},
{"green_blue_and_red_color": "The green, blue, and red colors are used in the graphical representation of the CNN structure to differentiate between various components of the network."},
{"green_blue_and_red_color__signify__the_kernel_size_max_pooling_and_fully_connected_layer_respectively": "The colors signify or represent different elements within the CNN architecture, providing a visual distinction between them."},
{"the_kernel_size_max_pooling_and_fully_connected_layer_respectively": "The kernel size, max-pooling, and fully connected layer are key components of the CNN architecture. The kernel size refers to the size of the filter used in the convolutional layers, max-pooling is a down-sampling technique used to reduce the dimensionality of each feature map, and the fully connected layer is where the actual classification or regression takes place."},
{"proposed_cnn_architecture": "The proposed CNN architecture is a deep learning model specifically designed for processing EEG signals. It is structured to handle input sample lengths of 4097, with various layers configured to extract and reduce features effectively."},
{"proposed_cnn_architecture__includes__five_convolutional_five_maxpooling_and_three_fully_connected_layers_total_of_eleven_layers_deep_cnn": "The proposed CNN architecture includes a specific configuration of layers designed to process and analyze EEG signals. This configuration is crucial for the model's ability to accurately interpret the complex data it receives."},
{"five_convolutional_five_maxpooling_and_three_fully_connected_layers_total_of_eleven_layers_deep_cnn": "The configuration includes five convolutional layers, which apply various kernel sizes to the input data to detect features; five maxpooling layers, which reduce the spatial dimensions of the feature maps; and three fully connected layers, which integrate the extracted features to make final predictions. This results in a total of eleven layers, making it a deep CNN."},
{"stride": "Stride refers to the step size with which the convolution operation moves across the input data in a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). It is a crucial parameter that affects the output dimensions and the computational efficiency of the network.|Here is another description: Stride is a parameter in convolutional neural networks that determines the step size at which the filter moves across the input signal during the convolution process."},
{"stride__is_set_at__1_and_2_for_convolution_of_the_eeg_signals_and_max_pooling_operations_respectively": "The phrase \"is set at\" indicates the specific configuration or value assigned to the stride parameter within the CNN architecture. This setting determines how the convolution and max-pooling operations are performed on the EEG signals."},
{"1_and_2_for_convolution_of_the_eeg_signals_and_max_pooling_operations_respectively": "The values \"1 and 2\" represent the stride settings for the convolution and max-pooling operations, respectively. A stride of 1 for convolution means the filter moves one pixel at a time, while a stride of 2 for max-pooling means the filter moves two pixels at a time, reducing the spatial dimensions of the feature maps."},
{"input_layer_layer_0_in_fig_4": "The input layer, designated as Layer 0 in Figure 4, serves as the initial point of data entry into the convolutional neural network (CNN). It represents the raw input data, in this case, EEG signals of 4097 sample lengths, before any processing or transformation."},
{"input_layer_layer_0_in_fig_4__is_convolved_using__equation_3_with_a_kernel_of_size_6_to_produce_layer_1": "The process of convolution involves applying a mathematical operation to the input data using a specific equation and kernel size. This operation transforms the input data into a new set of data, highlighting certain features and reducing dimensionality, which is essential for the subsequent layers to learn and extract more complex patterns."},
{"equation_3_with_a_kernel_of_size_6_to_produce_layer_1": "Equation (3) with a kernel of size 6 is used to perform the convolution operation on the input layer. This specific equation and kernel size are chosen to effectively process the EEG signals, producing Layer 1 of the CNN. The result of this convolution is a feature map that serves as the input for the next layer in the network."},
{"max_pooling_of_size_2": "Max-pooling of size 2 is a downsampling technique used in convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to reduce the spatial dimensions of feature maps. It involves selecting the maximum value from a 2x2 window and moving this window across the feature map.|Here is another description: Max-pooling of size 2 is a downsampling technique used in convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to reduce the spatial dimensions of feature maps. It involves selecting the maximum value from a subset of the feature map, which helps in retaining the most prominent features while reducing the computational load."},
{"max_pooling_of_size_2__is_applied_to__every_feature_map_layer_2": "The phrase \"is applied to\" indicates the action of implementing the max-pooling operation on a specific set of data within the CNN structure. This operation helps in reducing the computational load and controlling overfitting by decreasing the number of parameters."},
{"every_feature_map_layer_2": "Every feature map in Layer 2 refers to the collection of feature maps generated after the initial convolution operation in the CNN. These feature maps contain the extracted features from the input data, which are then subjected to max-pooling to further process and reduce their dimensions."},
{"number_of_neurons__is_reduced_from__4092_4_to_2046_4": "The phrase \"is reduced from\" indicates a decrease in the number of neurons as a result of a specific operation within the neural network, such as max-pooling."},
{"4092_4_to_2046_4": "The object \"4092 \u00d7 4 to 2046 \u00d7 4\" describes the change in the number of neurons after applying a max-pooling operation, where the number of neurons is halved from 4092 \u00d7 4 to 2046 \u00d7 4."},
{"feature_map_in_layer_2": "The feature map in Layer 2 is a set of data representations derived from the previous layer (Layer 1) after applying a convolution operation with a kernel of size 6. This feature map serves as the input for further processing in the convolutional neural network (CNN)."},
{"feature_map_in_layer_2__is_convolved_with__a_kernel_of_size_5_to_produce_layer_3": "The predicate \"is convolved with\" describes the process where the feature map in Layer 2 undergoes a convolution operation. This operation involves applying a mathematical function to the feature map using a specific kernel size to extract and emphasize certain features, leading to the production of the next layer in the CNN."},
{"a_kernel_of_size_5_to_produce_layer_3": "A kernel of size 5 is a small matrix used in the convolution operation to scan and process the feature map in Layer 2. This kernel moves across the feature map, performing element-wise multiplication and summation to produce a new feature map, which constitutes Layer 3 in the CNN structure."},
{"max_pooling_operation_of_size_2": "The max-pooling operation of size 2 is a technique used in convolutional neural networks to reduce the spatial dimensions of feature maps. It involves selecting the maximum value from a set of values in a specified window, which helps in retaining the most significant features while reducing the computational load."},
{"max_pooling_operation_of_size_2__is_applied_to__every_feature_map_layer_4": "The phrase \"is applied to\" indicates the action of implementing the max-pooling operation on a specific set of data. In this context, it signifies the process of performing the max-pooling operation on the feature maps generated in the convolutional neural network."},
{"every_feature_map_layer_4": "Every feature map in Layer 4 represents the output of a convolutional operation applied to the input data. These feature maps contain spatial information and patterns extracted by the convolutional filters, which are then subjected to the max-pooling operation to reduce their dimensions and highlight the most prominent features."},
{"number_of_neurons__is_reduced_to__1021_4": "The phrase \"is reduced to\" indicates a decrease in the number of neurons from one layer to the next within the CNN structure. This reduction typically occurs after a max-pooling operation, which is designed to downsample the feature maps, thereby reducing computational complexity and the risk of overfitting."},
{"1021_4": "The object \"1021 \u00d7 4\" represents the reduced number of neurons in a specific layer of the CNN after applying a max-pooling operation. This notation suggests that there are 1021 neurons in each of 4 feature maps, indicating a significant reduction from the previous layer's neuron count."},
{"feature_map_from_layer_4": "The feature map from Layer 4 is a set of data representations derived from the previous convolutional and pooling layers in the CNN architecture. It serves as the input for further processing in the network."},
{"feature_map_from_layer_4__is_convolved_with__a_kernel_of_size_4_to_produce_layer_5": "The operation \"is convolved with\" refers to the process where the feature map undergoes convolution, a mathematical operation that involves applying a kernel to the input to produce a transformed output. This is a fundamental step in convolutional neural networks for feature extraction."},
{"a_kernel_of_size_4_to_produce_layer_5": "A kernel of size 4 is a small matrix used in the convolution process. When convolved with the feature map from Layer 4, it produces Layer 5, which is another set of feature maps with extracted features at a higher level of abstraction."},
{"max_pooling_of_size_2__is_applied_to__reduce_the_number_of_neurons_in_the_output_layer_to_509_10_layer_6": "The phrase \"is applied to\" indicates the action of implementing the max-pooling operation on a specific layer within the CNN. This operation is crucial for reducing the dimensionality of the feature maps, which in turn helps in decreasing the number of parameters and computations in subsequent layers."},
{"reduce_the_number_of_neurons_in_the_output_layer_to_509_10_layer_6": "Reducing the number of neurons in the output layer to 509 \u00d7 10 (Layer 6) refers to the outcome of applying the max-pooling operation. This reduction helps in minimizing the complexity of the model, making it more efficient and less prone to overfitting, while still preserving essential information for the task at hand."},
{"feature_map_in_layer_6": "The feature map in Layer 6 represents the output of the previous convolutional and max-pooling operations in the CNN architecture. It is a set of data that has been transformed through multiple layers of convolution and pooling, reducing the dimensionality and extracting relevant features from the input EEG signals."},
{"feature_map_in_layer_6__is_convolved_with__a_kernel_size_of_4_to_produce_the_next_layer_layer": "The predicate \"is convolved with\" describes the process where the feature map in Layer 6 undergoes a convolution operation. This operation involves applying a kernel (a small matrix used for filtering) across the feature map to produce a new set of data, which forms the next layer in the CNN."},
{"a_kernel_size_of_4_to_produce_the_next_layer_layer": "A kernel size of 4 refers to the dimensions of the kernel used in the convolution operation. In this context, it is a 4x4 matrix that is applied to the feature map in Layer 6 to produce the next layer (Layer 7) in the CNN architecture. This kernel size determines the extent of the local region in the feature map that is considered during the convolution process."},
